## Contents
## 1. Introduction
Inter-linking inorganic nodes by appropriate directional linkers provide metal–organic frameworks (MOF) of varieties in porous architectures of different sizes and shapes [1]. Over the years, the subject has grown to get recognized as reticular chemistry [1c-d]. A large numbers of metal–organic frameworks are derived from dicarboxylates where coordination modes, linkers, connects and the secondary building blocks play roles to control sizes, shapes and porosities. The uses of different functional moiety in the dicarboxylate units modulate properties of MOFs. Conventionally, respective pores in pristine samples of MOFs are filled with solvent or guest molecules, but some MOFs are permanently porous. Upon desolvation certain MOFs also loss original architectures. Stability and hydrophobicity of MOFs are major concerns to implicate utilities for enhanced guest-bindings and for selective separations [2]. Accordingly, permanently porous stable hydrophobic MOFs are sought after. Certain naphthalenedicarboxylate (ndc) based MOFs have high thermal stability, also have pores of definite sizes and shapes with intrinsic hydrophobicity. These properties enable them to store inert gas [3a] and some of them have ability to serve as high-energy storage devices [3b]. On the other hand, to cause chemical reactions between reactants they should be able to diffuse into the pores; hence the pores must have suitable size as well as affinity for the reactants. To act as catalyst MOFs should have differentiable affinity for the products. These qualifications are fulfilled by certain ndc-MOFs and they act as catalyst for specific reactions [4]. On the other hand, the leaching of the MOF should be minimized to observe consistent and unwarranted reactions in heterogeneous catalysis. MOFs fulfilling such norms show superior selectivity and specificity in catalysis [5]. In certain cases, the pores of an MOF are partly or completely filled up or left vacant [6]. Furthermore, suitable orientations of functional units or parts of linkers to control intake and release of guest molecules acting like gates. Such a process is depicted in the Fig. 1 by taking an example of a cube constructed with naphthalene derived linkers with metal nodes. There are infinite numbers of orientations of naphthalene motif with different increments of orientations; but two extreme cases where all the naphthalene rings are protruding outwards or inside the cube is illustrated.
This aspect is important not only to make available space for inclusion of guest molecules but also for other opto-electronic properties associated due to different stacking among the naphthalene rings or with respect to similar or dissimilar aromatic rings. In an MOF, having voids or easily accessible voids by removing pre-adsorbed solvent molecules are better to facilitate uptake of guest molecules as compared to reorganising a tightly barricaded space to creat voids. In terms of adsorptions of gas/guest molecules, reversible opening and closing of pores of a porous material to modulate intake and release is referred as a gating process.
The MOFs based on naphthalenedicarboxylates also falls in the mainstream of MOFs showing versatile properties. There are many issues beyond identification of porous MOF to identify gated guest intake and release behaviour through modulations of pores. Controlling the occupancies of guests with varied sizes in these gated pores has practical utility in separation and storage. Accordingly, modulation of gated pores of naphthalene dicarboxylate-based MOFs were studied using in situ measurements; which included crystal to crystal transformations [6], NMR and Raman spectroscopy [7]. Different phases of naphthalenedicaroxylates including metastable phase have been structurally characterised. The electro-catalysis and energy-storage devices [8], electrical conductivity [9] are shown by dicarboxylate MOFs have received great attentions. Large variations of structural parameters and conditions to modulate properties and stabilities or decompositions of
MOFs, provided higher status as a deliverable topic to develop next-generation materials [1d–1e]. Those can be achieved by reticular synthesis by controlling linkers and nodes. The versatile coordination modes and ability form self-assembled structures provide scopes to make various self-assembled coordination polymers and MOFs having interesting material properties. Furthermore, use of aromatic moiety connected by C–C bond to carboxylate can provide mechanical motion through different orientation to provide mechanical motions. To a certain extent this aspect has been achieved by incorporating various functional characteristics to linker ligands and by varying metal ion connects. The polydentate linkers that are also possess variable binding parameters and their sizes being relatively bigger; provide special interests to explore them for with forecasted new methodologies. There is theme issue in Chemical Society Review from collections of many articles on MOFs appeared in RSC where the editorial description clearly mentions about the prospects on various aspects of MOFs [1b]. Intrinsic biodegradability of MOFs provides advantages over applied covalent molecules in biological applications [10], MOFs are applied for enzyme mimic [11], magnetic resonance imaging [12], computed tomography [13], molecular machines [14], sensing [1f,15].
Use of different linkers to defined nodes for control on properties is a challenge; hence, there is need to analyse and enlist the observed properties associated with different naphthalenedicarboxylate motifs. The Scheme 1 suggests one to search structural options to control orientations of the planar naphthalene units and also is suggestive to incorporate additional functional groups or coligands to have control on the orientations while they form frame-work structures. There are ten positional isomers of naphthalenedicarboxylic acids; which are listed in Scheme 1. Each provides independent type of directional scaffolds for a virgin area to carry out systematic studies to construct infinite numbers of MOFs.
Furthermore, the naphthalenedicarboxylate compounds also provide helical structures [16]; their interesting magnetic properties [17], photophysical [18], photochromic properties [19] are well known; which necessitates understanding on naphthalene based MOFs. Certain naphthalenedicarboxylate MOFs can separate individual component of acidic gases; for example, gases such as carbon dioxide, nitric oxide, sulphur dioxide and hydrogen sulphide were separated from their mixures in hydrocarbons [20]. This review is to decipher the creamy aspects of the new emerging properties of naphthalene dicaboxylate MOFs.
## 2. Self-assemblies of metal-naphthalenecarboxylates and coordination polymers
Carboxylate group binds to metal ions in various ways and such coordination modes listed in figure 2 are involved in selfassembling carboxylate complexes. The binding modes illustrated in the one side of dicarboxylate parts in the Fig. 2 are applicable to the other carboxylate group; similar or dissimilar combination of these binding modes by the two carboxylate groups decide the directional nature of naphthalenedicarboxylate complexes. The directional nature of carboxylate groups guides the structures of different types of self-assembled coordination polymers, metal–organic frameworks. Hence, it is necessary to understand the fundamental aspects of the coordination modes in self-assemblies and MOFs. Definite impacts on the structural aspects of carboxylate complexes are brought about by coligands and such understanding helps to predesign a synthesis. Hence, in this part, selected lownuclearity complexes of naphthalene mono and dicarboxylates are discussed to suggest their structural implications and reflections on magnetic properties. Since the binding of dicarboxylates are extensions of mono-carboxylates, the coordination of selfassembling of lower nuclearity complexes and selected coordination polymers of dicarboxylates are described here. Depending on the location of carboxylic acid on the naphthalene, the naphthalene carboxylic acid has two positional isomers; each provides scope to study self-assembling of their carboxylate complexes. For example, depending on co-ligands, aggregates of cadmium 1- or 2- naphthalenecarboxylic acid complexes are formed [21]. These can be retro-analysed to show that they are composed of selfassemblies of different neutral carboxylate complexes. Selfassembled coordination polymer [21a] of cadmium naphthalene2-carboxylate complex having 3,5-dimethylpyrazole or pyridine as coligands are shown in the Scheme 2. The self-assembly of the
Scheme 2. Different self-assembled cadmium-naphthalene-2-carboxylate coordination polymers possessing (a) 3,5-dimethyl pyrazole and (b) pyridine as coligands.
coordination polymer having 3,5-dimethylpyrazole as coligand has $\upmu^{2}$ -carboxylate bridges forming $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}({\mathsf C}{\mathsf O}_{2})_{2}$ motifs; which were assembled to provide ${\mathsf{C d}}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}_{2}$ motifs. The $\mathrm{Cd}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ motifs are generally formed as a consequence of side-on $\upmu^{1}$ -oxy bridge present between two carboxylates. These bridges yield eight coordination environments around cadmium ions. When pyridine is the coligand, the self-assembly is formed through hydrogen bonding of the coordinated water molecule of one complex with carboxylates of other neighbouring complex. These types of understandings provide basis for a bottom up build-up of these complexes from different combinations of neutral complexes.
Binuclear oxide-bridged iron(III) 1,4,7-trimethyl-1,4,7-triazacy clononane $(\mathrm{{tacn-Me}}_{3})$ ) 2-naphthalenecarboxylate complex $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{F e}_{2}(0)$ $(\mathsf{O}_{2}\mathsf{C C H}_{2}\mathsf{C}_{10}\mathsf{H}_{7})_{2}(\mathsf{t a c n}-\mathsf{M e}_{3})_{2}](\mathsf{P F}_{6})_{2}$ shown in Fig. 3 upon photoexcitation of the naphthalene moiety, formed an excimer with a relatively short life-time (4.6 ns) [22]. This occurred due to non-cofacial geometry between the naphthalene rings in the complex. The excimer occurred as a consequence of the orientation of the naphthalene group conferred by the oxide-biscarboxylate scaffold by which they were held. It was also found that the excimer emission was not effected by variations of concentration, which ruled out a possibility of a bimolecular quenching. In the literature it was also reported that co-facial arrangements of aromatic planes of poly-aromatics such as of 9,10-dimethyl anthracene has higher lifetime $(\sim220\ \mathrm{ns})$ whereas two oblique arrangements between two anthracene planes ${\sim}55^{\circ}$ produce an excimer having lifetime 6 ns [23]. Such issues are expected in MOFs possessing naphthalene based linkers; as the nodes of an MOF would guide the orientations of such rings on linkers. Thus, in the framework structures with organised aromatic rings would have a major role to dictate their emission properties.
Beyond the structural aspects, interests on these complexes are from point of view of materials. For example, ferroelectric and antiferroelectric phases are associated with liquid crystals of (R)-1- methylheptyl-6- ( $\mathbf{{\dot{4}}^{\prime}}$ -decycloxybenzoyloxy)-2-naphthalene carboxylate [24]. Single molecular magnet property was observed in fluorescent trinuclear manganese 2-naphthalenecarboxylate complexes possessing oximate coligands [25]. The rhenium complex $[(2\mathrm{-nca})\mathrm{Re}(\mathrm{CO})_{3}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipy})]$ $2{\cdot}\mathrm{nc}\mathsf{a}\ =$ naphthalene-2-carboxylate, $2,2^{\prime}{\mathrm{-bpy}}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine) showed dual emissions due to metal to ligand charge transfer and a long-lived ligand-to-ligand charge transfer transition [26]. Due to the suitable orientations of the fluorophoric parts in hetero-metallic complex of sodium and europium ions possessing mixed ligands, namely, 2-naphthalenecarboxylate and $\upbeta$ -diketonate was found to be highly fluorescent. On subtle variations of temperatures, the emission intensity showed by the complex changes, which makes it a potential candidate of luminescence-based thermometer for biological studies [27].
Coordination polymers of 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylates were formed with diverse topologies as consequences of coordination of co-ligands, coordination of water and association of guest molecules [29]. For example, coordination polymers $\{[\mathsf{M n}_{2}(1,4-$ $\mathrm{ndc)_{2}(p h e n)_{2}](H_{2}O)\}_{n}$ and $\{[\mathsf{M n}_{4}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(\mathsf{p h e n})_{4}](\mathsf{D M F})_{2}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (phen $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1.10$ -phenanthroline; DMF $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ N,N-dimethylformamide) each had two-dimensional (4,4)-connected grid-like network [28] as shown in Fig. 4 (a) and (b), whereas $[\mathrm{Mn}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{phen})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ had a three-dimensional structure (Fig. 4c).
These coordination polymers were prepared by varying the identical reactant but used in different concentrations of all the reactants and it was suggested that decreasing amount of the coligand concentration allows water molecules to be part of coordination sphere to influence the structures. Single molecule of the bidentate phenanthroline ligand was present in each SBU from reaction at lower concentration of phen, this provided higher flexibility to the carboxylate part to form a 3D linkage topology by the coordination polymer $\mathrm{\Delta[Mn_{2}(1,4-n d c)_{2}(p h e n)(H_{2}O)]_{n}}$ . Weak intradimer antiferromagnetic interaction and very weak inter-dimer interactions in the two water containing coordination polymers were observed as a consequence of limitations offered by requirements on distance and overlap of magnetic orbitals; whereas the DMF containing coordination polymer due to syn–anti carboxylate bridges do not facilitate overlaps between magnetic orbitals of Mn (II) ions. On the other hand, the three-dimensional coordination polymer had antiferromagnetic coupling between the $\mathsf{M n}(\mathrm{II})$ ions suggesting the dimensionality guiding the magnetic interactions. This is indicative of the fact the orientations of the carboxylate groups with respect to one-another as well as the enhancement of proximal reach between the metal ions plays key role in deciding magnetic properties of these coordination polymers.
The 1,4-ndc based coordination polymer $\mathrm{[Ni_{4}(1,4-}$ $\mathrm{ndc)_{3}(O H)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}.2.5H_{2}O]_{n}}$ has a pcu topology [28] (Fig. 5); it showed metamagnetic behaviour; whereas two anionic coordination polymers with having 1-pentyl-3-methylimidazolium (MIm) cations encapsulated namely, $\{[\mathsf{M n}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathsf{a c e t a t e})](\mathsf{M I m})\cdot0\cdot.5$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}{\mathsf{O}}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ , possess Ion topology whereas $\{[\mathsf{C o}_{3}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})4(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}]$ $(\mathrm{MIm})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ possessing bcu topology were found to be antiferromagnetic.The structural features changes depending on metal ions, coordination modes and the nodes (Fig. 5), these also influence the magnetic interactions to show different magnetic properties.
Conformational flexibilities conferred by flexible coligands influence the structures and compositions of 1,4- naphthalenedicarboxylate coordination polymers. For examples use the bis-pyridyl bis amide based ligands bpba4 and bpba6 listed in Fig. 6 adopted different forms in coordination polymers; which also makes impact on the coordination modes of carboxylates of 1,4-ndc linkers. Due to change in conformation different topologies were observed by changing reaction conditions [30a]. The cadmium coordination polymers $\{[\mathsf{C d}(\mathsf{b b b a6})(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})]\cdot2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\}_{n}$ , having the bpda ligand adopted 4,5T61 topology and $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{2}$ (bpda6) $(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}]\cdot3\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\}_{n}$ , showed sqc776 topology (Fig. 6). These two coordination polymers were reversibly interconverted by changing the crystallisation medium from namely from water or ethanol. In fact, the difference in the two structures arose due to change of coordination modes and the conformational changes. These changes required minimum volume change and required conformational changes in the coordination modes due to change in compositions and were guided by the coordination of water molecules Based on method of prepartions interesting coordination polymers with new topologies were identified [30]. Infinite numbers of structural variations can be imagined with flexible ligands to form new self-assemblies. For example, the self-assembled structures of 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate having coligands N,N0-dimethyletha nolamine (Hdmea), N-methyldiethanolamine (dmea), Nbutyldiethanolamine (Hbdea), or $\mathsf{N},\mathsf{N},\mathsf{N}^{\prime},\mathsf{N}^{\prime}$ -tetrakis(2-hydroxye thyl)ethylenediamine (H4etda) form varieties of self-assemblies different topologies [30b]. Topologies of these were interesting; for example, the coordination polymer $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{dmea})_{2}(\upmu-2,6-\upmu$ dc) $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\mathrm{ln}{\cdot}2\mathrm{nH}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ , self-assembled through lattice water by transforming from $\ensuremath{1\mathrm{D}}\ensuremath{\rightarrow}3\ensuremath{\mathrm{D}}$ to adopt topology sqc513; whereas $[\mathsf{C u2}$ $(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Hmdea})_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-}2,6\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ndc})]\mathrm{n}{\cdot}2\mathrm{n}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ transforms from $10~\rightarrow~3\mathsf{D}$ to adopt trinodal 3,4,6-nets. On the other hand, $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(\upmu\mathrm{-Hbdea})_{2}(\upmu-$ $2,6\mathrm{-ndc)}|\mathrm{n}{\cdot}2\mathrm{n}\mathrm{H}_{2}0,$ , and $\mathrm{[Cu_{2}(H4e t d a)_{2}(\upmu-2,6-n d c)]{\cdot n d a}.4H_{2}}$ $\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ were build up by $10\rightarrow2\mathrm{D}$ adopting 3,4,6L6 topology, or $\mathsf{O D}\rightarrow3\mathsf{D}$ 3,4,8-connected nets respectively. These analyses provided simplified supramolecular self-assembling through lattice water, but exposed the complexity in substrate specific structural variations in these complexes which made it difficult to arrive at generalised formulations for designed synthesis.
## 3. Metal organic frameworks of naphthalenedicarboxylates
As mentioned in Scheme 1, the ten positional isomers of naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (ndc) provide opportunities to study them or their derivatives as directional linkers. Hence, naphthalenenedicarboxylates are open-ended scaffolds to bring about variations in directionalities and shapes of an MOF. Due to multiple binding modes of carboxylates, complicacies to connect metal or secondary building units occur, furthermore, the presence of coligands adds diversities to structures. Thus, positional isomer of ndc is as important as the coligands and metals ions in a node for designed architectures. The obvious reasons to prepare a MOF of predictable geometry from ndc are their structural simplicities and easy commercial availability. Two carboxylic acid groups located at positioned at wide apart locations in a near opposite direction $(\sim180^{\circ})$ with respect to each other, are suitable as linker for construction of gridded MOFs with high predictability. So, the obvious choices for such MOFs are linkers from 1,4; 2,6; 2,7 and 1,6- naphthalenedicarboxylate isomers. Accordingly, large numbers of MOFs have been synthesised from these carboxylates, which are the content of this review. Though, relatively lesser numbers of examples of MOFs or coordination polymers based on $1,2\mathrm{-};2,3\mathrm{-}$ and 2,7-naphthalenedicarboxylic acids are there, yet the existing examples have been contributing to literature. Different MOFs of different isomeric naphthalenedicarboxylates are discussed in the sub-sections to follow.
## 3.1. Metal 1,2- and 2,3-naphthalenedicarboxylates
1,2- and 2,3-naphthalenedicarboxylic acids, have structural similarity by having two carboxylic groups next to each other. The orientations are not favourable for MOF but more suitable to act as linkers for coordination polymers. In certain cases, such polymers self-assemble to form three dimensional architectures. The 1,2-naphthalenedicarboxylate (1,2-ndc) forms 1-D chains [31] having $\mathsf{C d}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{2}$ secondary building units; whereas, having a coligand such as 1,3-bis(4-pyridyl)propane (bpp), and $^{4,4^{\prime}-}$ bipyridine (bpy) together with ndc resulted pentanuclear secondary building unit $\mathrm{[Cd_{5}(\mu_{3}{-}O H)_{2}(C O O)_{2}}$ . This pentanuclear building unit was found in 1D chains of coordination polymers, such as $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{5}(1,2\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(\mathsf{b p p})_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}](\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{4}\}_{n}$ and $\mathrm{[Cd_{5}(1,2-}$ ndc) $\mathsf{\iota}_{4}(\mathsf{b p y})_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}\vert_{n}$ . Ring opening reactions of 1,2- naphthalenedicarboxylic anhydride in the presence or absence of a coligand were used instead of a direct reaction of 1,2- $\operatorname{H}_{2}{\bmod{\mathrm{c}}}$ with metal salt to prepare different cyclic and acyclic motifs. The use of 1,2-bis(4-pyridine)ethane or trans-1,2-(4- pyridyl)ethylene as coligands with 1,2-ndc had provided interpenetrating polymeric frameworks having carboxylate bridged $Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(1,2\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}$ binuclear nodes [32]. Different types of nodes in copper 2,3-naphthalenedicarboxylate coordination polymers [Cu (2,3-ndc) $|(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cal O})|_{\mathrm{n}},$ $\mathrm{[Cu(2,3-ndc)(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{n}}$ and $\{[\mathsf{C u}_{1.5}(2,3\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathrm{OH})$ $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]0.2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ were observed, which provided versatility to generate new architectures [33].
## Quinoline/sodium hydroxide
Similarly, varieties of silver and zinc nodes in metal-2,3-naph thalenedicarboxylates have prospects for bottom up synthesis for higher analogues [34]. Wide differences in fluorescence emissions in these complexes were observed due to intra-ligand charge transfer emissions. Fluorescent properties of trinuclear lanthanide 2,3-ndc coordination polymers possessing 1,10-phenanthroline as coligand depend on the central metal ion [35]. In the case of $\mathsf{D y}^{3\bar{+}}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ conventional fluorescence emission due to f-block ions were observed; whereas, upon excitation with UV radiation, the ${\sf E u}^{3+}$ complex showed luminescence in near infra-red region. Corresponding $\mathsf{D y}^{3+}$ complex exhibited slow magnetic relaxation at low temperature, hence possesses single molecule magnet property.
Besides these 2,3-naphthalene scaffold derived flexible ligand naphthalene-2,3-diyldioxy-di-acetate was [36] used to stabilise seven coordinated complexes and to prepare varieties of coordination polymers. Different types of cyclic complexes and linear coordination polymers were formed depending on the coligands. Certain scaffolds had macrocyclic units, which provided adequate spaces within macrocyclic scaffolds for recognition of sodium ions. Two illustrative examples of hetero-metallic and homo-metallic coordination polymers [36a] are illustrated in Fig. 7. Two different types of nickel 2,3-ndc coordination polymers were formed using the same reagents from reactions in quinoline or in pyridine. In the case of quinoline solvent, water molecules competed to coordinate and occupied the coordination sphere of nickel ions. It did not allow quinoline to coordinate, which has weak coordinating ability. This helped to form macrocyclic scaffolds to recognise sodium ions. Hence, sodium cations were trapped in the macrocyclic motifs of the coordination polymer (Fig. 7a). Pyridine being a better ligand when it coordinated to nickel ion, resulted a linear coordination polymer devoid of sodium ion (Fig. 7b). There are examples based on naphthalene-2,7-diyldioxy-di-acetate coordination polymers showing non-linear optical properties [36c]; whereas, the naphthalene-1,4-diyldioxy-di-acetate based MOF encapsulating complex cation [36d] and coordination polymer changing dimensionality [36e] with change of solvents were reported in literature.
## 3.2. 1,4-Naphthalenedicarboxylate based MOFs
Two carboxylate groups of 1,4-ndc ligand are at $180^{\circ}$ angle with respect to each other; and is analogous to the most sought after connect namely benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate also known as terephthalate. Each of the 1,4-ndc based MOF has the ability to show different properties depending on the coligands, reaction conditions, cations etc.; some features of such are highlighted in tabular form in Table 1 and Table 2. Out of different metal frameworks the one having demonstrated applications are listed in Table 1 where classifications are made from the metal ions of alkali, alkaline earth metal ions and first row transition metal complexes. The other metal ion based 1,4-ndc are listed in Table 2. Different SBUs are observed in magnesium MOFs of 1,4-ndc [37], namely $\mathrm{[Mg(1,4-}$ ndc)(dmi)] and $[\mathrm{Hdmu\cdotMg_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc)_{2}(0H)(d m u)_{4}}}]$ where ${\mathrm{d}}{\mathrm{mi}}=1$ , 3-dimethyl-2-imidazolidinone; dmu $\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}\mathsf{N}$ $\mathsf{N}^{\prime}$ -dimethylurea; each has different types of SBU as illustrated in Fig. 8. The $\scriptstyle{\mathrm{{Mg}}}(1,4-$ ndc)(dmi) has paddle-wheel carboxylate bridged dimeric units and possesses a layer-like structure. Whereas, the SBU of the [Hdmu $\mathrm{\cdotMg_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(O H)(d m u)_{4}]}$ has an oxide-bridge and two carboxylate bridges in binuclear units.
Calcium MOF of 1,4-ndc possessing a coordinated dimethylformamide (DMF) per calcium ion Ca(1,4-ndc)(DMF) shows nonlinear optical properties [38], it has helical chains of same chirality with periodicity along the crystallographic $a$ -axis. Ionothermal synthesis [39] performed in ionic liquid 1-propylene-3- methylimidazolium chloride (amimCl) as solvent resulted incorporation of the imidazolium component in anionic three-dimensional framework [a $\mathrm{\mim]_{2}[M g_{3}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(m e i m)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}].2H_{2}O}$ (meim $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 1-methylimidazole). This magnesium MOF possesses trinuclear magnesium clusters as secondary building units. Furthermore, dimethyl ammonium cations included in 1D channels observed within a magnesium MOF [40] is a turn-off sensor of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ . Same MOF is also selective sensitizer of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ ions; showed intense pure red emission. Aluminium based MOF has drawn interest by showing glutamic acid inclusion [41a] selectively over several other biological molecules. It had also shown selective hydrogen and methane gas absorptions [41b]. Membranes of MOF, [Al(OH)(1,4- ndc)] were prepared in microwave conditions; these membranes were useful for adsorption of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ ; selectivity ratio shown by the membrane for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ was 13.3. This value on selectivity was higher than 8.6 that were observed for selectivity ratio of absorption between $\mathsf{H}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ gases by the MOF in conventional crystalline condition [41b].
Among the manganese based MOFs, $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{M n}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ has two six coordinated independent $\mathrm{{Mn}(I I)}$ ions in distorted octahedral environments having the oxygen atoms coonnecting each manganese ion [42a]. Manganese ions contributed to form square grid-like structures and corner sharing of those octahedrons constructed a three dimensional network. Whereas, the dimethylacetamide containing MOF have five-coordinated manganese ions, each manganese ion in trigonal–bipyramidal coordination environment. In this case the 1D-chains were connected to four other chains to make a three dimensional architecture [42]. On the other hand, MOF of iron with 1,4-ndc, $\mathrm{\Delta[Fe(OH)(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})]{\cdot2H_{2}O}},$ was identified as a good absorber of carbon dioxide [43a]. The isostructural 1,4-ndc MOFs of vanadium, chromium, namely, [V(OH)(1,4-ndc)] and $\left[\mathrm{Cr(OH)}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc)}\right]$ , were also structurally characterised. Due to large dimension of voids in the channels of these MOFs, they acted as hosts for metal-oxide-inserted nano porous carbonaceous materials [43b]. Two types of channels were found in the MOF, [Ga (OH)(1,4-ndc)] (Fig. 9); it has channel with $5.5\times5.5\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ which was a relatively smaller diameter. These channels were located along the $c$ -crystallographic axis; whereas, another set of channels with larger pores were with $9\times9\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ pore-diameter [43].
Varieties of nodes observed in cobalt(II)-1,4-ndc MOFs were dependent on the reaction conditions and coligands. Antiferromagnetic interactions among the ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{I I}})$ ions in cobalt-1,4-ndc coordination polymer possessing $[\mathsf{C o}_{4}(\upmu_{2}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})_{2}(\mathsf{C O O})_{6}]$ secondary building units linked to 1,3-di(4-pyridyl)propane coligand (dpp) were observed [44a]. Catalytic activity of a three-dimensional $C o^{2+}$ -metal organic framework, $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}]$ was cyanosilylation reaction was compared with the cadmium analogues of the MOF having identical composition the cadmium MOF was found to be effective for shape selective cyanosilylation reaction. The catalytic reaction was performed to prepare cyanohydrin trimethylsilyl ethers from nitro aromatic aldehydes [44b]. In an another article, it was shown that the MOF, $\scriptstyle{\left[\mathsf{C o}_{2}\left(1,4-\right.\right.}$ $\mathrm{ndc)_{2}(a b b)(H_{2}O)_{2}(\mu_{2}-H_{2}O)]\cdot(D M F)_{2}(H_{2}O)\}_{n}$ $\mathbf{\hat{a}b b}=3{,}3^{\prime}$ -azo-bispyri dine) was an efficient catalyst conversion of carbon dioxide to cyclic carbonates, cyanosilylation and Knoevenagel condensation [44c]. These results suggested that the importance of the choice of linkers of MOFs for catalytic studies; as the latter cobalt MOF was active catalyst fir cyanosilylation whereas the former was not.
The cobalt based MOF, $\mathrm{\Delta[Co_{6}(\mu_{3}-O H)_{4}(1,4-n d c)_{4}(b i p)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{n}}$ [bip $\begin{array}{r l}{=}&{{}\ 1,1^{\prime}.}\end{array}$ -(1,5-pentanediyl)bis(1H-1,2,4-triazole)] possesses $\left[\mathsf{C o}_{6}(\upmu_{3}{-}0\mathsf{H})_{4}\right]^{8+}$ chains. The chains are comprised of alternate $\mathrm{[Co_{4}(O H)_{2}]^{6+}}$ and $\left[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}\right]^{2+}$ subunits. The MOF showed weak antiferromagnetic interactions between the adjacent $C o^{2+}$ ions [44d]. The MOFs, $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{m}\mathrm{d}\mathsf{p})_{2}]$ and [(Co(1,4-ndc)) $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\bmod{\mathrm{p}}}\right)]$ $\begin{array}{r l}{{\bf H}_{2}\mathbf{m}\mathbf{d}\mathbf{p}}&{{}~=}\end{array}$ methylenebis(3,5-dimethylpyrazole)
crystallized as a racemic twin and the observed features was interesting, as it provided a three fold 3D/3D heterointerpenetrating diamondoid framework with left-handed and right-handed helical chains [16a]. The $\mathrm{Co_{2}(d m i m p y m)(1,4-n d c)_{2}}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ (dmimpym $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 4,6-di(2-methyl-imidazol-1-yl)-pyrimidine) MOF exhibited ligand-based blue emission in the solid state. The increase in intensity by aluminium ion selectively enabled to detect ${\mathbb{A}}^{3+}$ with a limit of detection $0.7~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ [44e]. These examples clearly suggested that the utilities of each 1,4-ndc MOF were dictated by the colignads and provides avenues to make large numbers of structural variations to explore new properties.
Drastic changes in topology were observed in two 1,4-ndc derived MOF of nickel and zinc with same coligand. Difference in extent of protonated species of the coligand involved in MOF $\mathrm{[Ni_{3}(H h d b)_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(H_{2}O)_{2}]_{n}}$ and $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}(\mathsf{h d b})_{2}(1,4\mathsf{-n d c})_{2}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ [where hbd $~=~10^{\phantom{-}}$ -(1,6-hexanediyl)bis(imidazole)] were observed but the nodes and topologies were also completely independent of each other in those cases. The nickel MOF adopted a 3D CsCl-type topology whereas the 2D parallel interpenetration was observed in the corresponding cadmium MOF [45].
It has been possible to change the type of magnetic interactions among the $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ions as antiferromagnetic and ferromagnetic interactions of a series of Ni-MOFs by causing variations in the nodes by using geminal dicationic ionic liquids with different alkyl spacer lengths. The MOFs formulated as $\mathrm{[L_{1}][N i(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})B r]_{2}}$ and $[(\mathsf{L}_{2})_{2}]$ $[\mathsf{N i}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})\mathsf{B r}]_{2}$ , $\left[\mathrm{Ni_{3}}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(\mathrm{L}_{2})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{2}\right]$ and $\mathrm{[Ni}_{3}(1,4-$ ndc) $)_{4}(\mathrm{L}_{4})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]$ [L represents $^{3,3^{\prime}}$ -(1,n-alkanediyl)bis(1-methyli midazolium where alkane $\mathbf{\tau}=\mathbf{\tau}$ propane (L1, $\mathrm{~n~}=\ 3;$ , butane $(\mathrm{L}_{2}$ ${\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n}}}=4{\dot{\boldsymbol{\mathrm{\Omega}}}}$ , pentane $\left(\mathrm{L}_{3},\mathrm{n}=5\right),$ ), hexane $\left(\mathrm{L}_{4},\mathrm{n}=5\right).$ ) by changing the intervening $-\big(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{-}\big)_{\mathrm{n}}$ unit. When ${\mathfrak{n}}=3$ and 4, two-dimensional structures were observed, whereas $\mathrm{~n~}=\ 5$ and 6 adopted the 3Dnetworks (Fig. 10). The orientations of the alkyl chains were found to differ in the two types of structures showing possibility to modulate space in the interior of MOF by cations. The ones with dimeric paddle wheel SBU with the 2D networks were antiferromagnetic, whereas the ones with $\mathrm{~n~}=\mathrm{~5~}$ and 6 having linear carboxylate bridges were ferromagnetic [46a]. Dominant ferromagnetic interaction was observed in a 3D-networks constituted by 1D-chains of coordination polymer $\{\mathrm{Ni_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc)_{2}(4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-dpdo)(H_{2}O)].3H_{2}O\}_{n}}}$ (4,4ˊ-dpdo $\mathit{\Theta}=\mathit{\Theta}4,4^{\prime}$ -dipyridyl-N,Nˊ-dioxide). In the 3D-network antiferromagnetic/ferromagnetic interactions were observed among the alternating manganese ions [46b]. Chiral MOFs have intersts in optical inductions, non-linear optical properties, one such chiral copper 1,4-ndc mixed metal MOF containining sodium and copper ions $\left[(\mathrm{emim})\mathrm{NaCu}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}\right]_{\mathrm{n}}$ was prepared by ionothermal reaction in 1-ethyl-3-methylimidazolium tetrafluoroborate (emim-BF4) solvent. The framework structure of the
MOF had spiral carboxylate bridged chains with $\scriptstyle4_{1}$ topology provides chirality to the MOF as illustrated at the bottom part of the Fig. 11. This is an example of a chiral MOF formed from achiral precursors. The shortest $\mathtt{C u\mathrm{...Cu}}$ distances in this MOF is $6.61\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ ; hence, it is weakly anti-ferromagntic [47].
From structural point of view ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ - 1,4-ndc MOFs, such as $\mathrm{Cu}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{dabc}$ o) $(\mathrm{ndc}=\mathrm{dabco}=1,4\$ -diazobicyclo[2.2.2]octan e),[48a] [Cu(en)(1,4-ndc)](imida) $\left(\operatorname{H}_{2}0\right)$ $_\mathrm{en}=1,2$ -ethylenediamine, imida $\mathbf{\lambda}=\mathbf{\lambda}2$ -imidazolidinone, [48b] $[\mathsf{C u}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(3\mathrm{-bpye})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})]$ $[\mathsf{C u}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})(3\mathrm{-bpfp})_{0.5}],$ $\mathrm{[Cu_{3}(1,4-n d c)_{3}(3-b p c d)_{3}]\cdot2H_{2}O}$ and [Cu (1,4-ndc)(3-bpcb)], (where 3- $\mathsf{b p y e~=~N},\mathsf{N}^{\prime}{\mathrm{-bis}}$ (3-pyridinecarboxa mide)-1,2-ethane; 3-bpfp $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ bis(3-pyridylformyl)piperazine; 3-bpc $\mathbf{d}=\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime}$ -bis(pyridin-3-yl)cyclohexane-1,4-dicarboxamide; and 3- bpcb $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ N,N’-bis(3-pyridinecarboxamide)-1,4-benzene) [48c] $\mathrm{\Delta[Cu_{4}(1,4-n d c)_{4}(i m i d a n a p)_{2}]{\cdot}H_{2}O{\cdot}2D M F}$ , (imidanap $\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathbf{\Psi}1$ -(1-(1H-imi dazol-1yl)naphthalen-4-yl)-1H-imidazole) [48d] were structurally characterised. The MOF, $\{[\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{\textmu_{3}-O H})(\mathrm{\mu-Py}z)_{3}(1,4\mathrm{-Hndc})],$ DMF $\mathrm{\cdotH_{2}O C H_{3}O H\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $(\mathrm{HPyz}=\mathrm{Hpyrazole})$ [48e] is an example of a ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ -MOF in which using 1,4-ndc as the principal linker and keeping the metal ion same, variations of metal–ligand ratios could be brought about depending introducing coligands. This aspect generates interest as such changes, would be useful to tune properties associated with an MOF through reticular synthesis. Some of the copper-1,4-ndc MOFs were proved to be useful in photo-catalytic degradation of dyes [48d,48e,48f]; and also were used in controlled inclusion of guest molecules [48a]. Depending on the reaction conditions different compositions of copper-1,4-ndc MOF were observed from reactions of 1,4-ndc with copper salts. For example, competetive binding of methanol with water and also due to competetive deprotonation of water ligand yielded different ndc based MOFs. Such MOFs possesing either methanol, water or hydroxide or oxide ligands occupying the coordination sphere of the corresponding metal ions have wide difference in SBUs. Two similar MOFs, one possesing methanol in coordination sphere wheras another having water molecule in coordination sphere instead of methanol namely the MOFs, $\mathrm{[Cu(1,4-ndc)(H_{2}O)].3H_{2}O}$ and $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C u}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathsf{M e O H})].\mathsf{M e O H},$ showed large structural differences. The differences in the structures were reflected in the inclinations of the grids within the MOFs. The MOFs adsorbs different amounts of guest molecules and provided examples of structurally interchangeable new flexible MOFs. The structural flexibility in the two MOFs also allowed stepwise solvent sorption. The methanol containing MOF had shown gated sorption behaviour with MeOH, to have completely excluded $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ molecules [49]. The observed specificity was due to the hydrophilic and hydrophobic nature of the pores in the channels of these MOFs. The $\mathrm{[Cu(1,4-ndc)(H_{2}O)]}.3\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ MOF had both hydrophobic and hydrophilic channels as illustrated in Fig. 12a, of which hydrophilic channels were occupied by water molecules. Whereas, the methanol containing MOF had triangular hydrophobic channels (Fig. 12b); those were filled with coordinated methanol molecules as well as solvent of crystallisation methanol molecules.
Due to versatility in nodes, biocompatible nature and ease of synthesis as well as degradation, make zinc MOF of great interest in reticular chemistry. Among the zinc-1,4-ndc MOFs, the MOF, [Zn(1,4-ndc)(bcbpy)] 0.5DMF $_{(3\cdot.5\mathrm{H}_{2}0)}$ [bcbpy $\mathit{\Theta}=1,1$ -bis(4-carboxy benzyl)-4, ´4 -bipyridinium bis(hexa fluorophosphates)] discovered by Kitagawa and coworkers [50] provided new information on hydrogen gas adsorptions by the MOF. The interesting part of this MOF was that it was having an electroactive and zwterionic linker 1,1-bis(4-carboxybenzyl)-4,4´ -bipyridinium in the MOF. They have used techniques like $^{13}{\mathrm{CNMR}}$ spectroscopy, variable temperature infra-red spectroscopy and adsorption studies to understand hydrogen storage ability of the MOF [50]. The IR study carried out with the guest free MOF under hydrogen atmosphere at different pressure is shown in Fig. 13. On the basis of the control experiments it was suggested that the hydrogen gas adsorbed on the active sites on the interior surface. The IR spectra showed
$4095~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $4088~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were due to para- and orthohydrogen, respectively. The presence of only three peaks at
$4112\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $4095~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , $4088~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and lack of peaks in the region between $4040~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $4000\ c\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ , suggested that there were no chemi-sorptive sites in the MOF. The wide variations of structures were observed in MOFs like [Zn(1,4-ndc)(phenim)] [51a] (phenim $=\ 2$ -phenylimidazole), $[\mathrm{Zn}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathrm{butim})]0.0.25\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ [51a] [butim $\mathit{\Theta}=1,1\$ 0(1,4-butanediyl)bis(imidazole)], {[Zn(bipy)(1,4- ndc) $(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}0\}$ [51b](bipy $\mathit{\Pi}=\ 4{,}4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine), $\{[\mathsf{Z n}(\mathsf{b p e})(1,4-$ ndc) $\left]_{2}\right.$ 2DMF}[51b] (bpe $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1{,}2$ -bis(4pyridyl)ethane); $\{[Z\boldsymbol{\mathrm{n(bpee)}}$ (1,4-ndc)]22DMF}[51b] [bpee $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ 1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene]; $\mathrm{Zn}_{6}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{5}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{DM}$ F, [51c] $\{[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{bbib})_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{3}]$ $2\mathrm{DMF}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cal O}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ , [51d] (bbib $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}_{1,3}$ bis(benzimidazolyl)benzene), [51e] $\{[\mathrm{Zn_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc)_{2}(3\mathrm{-abpt)}]{\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}}}}_{\mathrm{n}}$ , [51] (3-abpt $=4$ -amino-3, 5-bis(3-pyridyl)-1,2,4-triazole), $\mathrm{[Zn_{4}(n d c)_{3}(O H)_{2}](C_{2}H_{5}O H)}$ $(1.4\mathrm{H}_{2}0)$ [51f]. The $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}](\mathrm{C_{2}H}_{5}\mathrm{OH})(1\cdot.4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ showed violet emission [51f] upon excitation by UV-radiations with large Stoke’s shift. The photoluminescence behaviour shown MOFs had helped them to act as sensors for analytes, The zincMOF, $\{[\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{bbib})_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}]{\cdot2\mathrm{DMF}\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ senses acetylacetone [51d], with a detection limit of $0.10\ \mathrm{ppm}$ ; the $\{[\mathsf{Z n}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(3\mathrm{-}$ abpt)] 2DMF}n selectively sensed [51e] ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions. Search of robust SBUs are of great interest, in a recent study $\scriptstyle\{[Z\cap_{7}(1,4-$ ndc) $)_{5.5}(\upmu_{4}\mathrm{-OH})_{3}]\cdot7\mathrm{DMF}\upmu$ possessing heptanuclear $\mathrm{Zn}_{7}(0\mathrm{H})_{3}(\mathrm{CO}_{2})_{11}$ cluster as secondary building unit was investigated. This MOF acted as a catalyst for photo-catalytic decomposition of dyes such as rhodamine B and methyl violet and was a sensor for iron(III) with detection limit of $1.16~\mathrm{ppm}$ [51f].
Local dynamics of naphthalene rings in the solid state was observed in the stimuli responsive 1,4-ndc MOF having 1,2-bis(4-pyridyl)ethane (bpe), namely $\{[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(\mathrm{bpe})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{solvent}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ [51g]. It has $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{COO^{-}})_{4}]$ paddle-wheel type secondary building units. The structural changes caused the stimuli such as such as temperature, pressure and by guest molecules were reflected in the carbon dioxide adsorptions by the MOF under different conditions. Two distinct desolvated phases were isolated from the MOF, between these states there was one metastable state which was transformed to the desolvate stable phase upon heating. Changing the linker from ethylene $\left(-\mathsf{C H}_{2}\mathbf{-CH}_{2}\mathbf{-}\right)$ to an azo $(-N=N-)$ functionality influenced the gate-opening pressure due to difference in internet interactions of these MOFs.
Cadmium is a popular metal ion for designing MOFs, it provides higher coordination numbers and also a good numbers of single crystals cadmium-1,4-ndc MOFs could be obtained to facilitate their chractersations [52a]. Using the same coligand wide variations in compositions of Cd-MOFs and the stabilisation of either of trans or cis conformers were reflected in different cadmium MOFs. The 1,4-ndc based MOFs, namely $\{[\mathsf{C d}_{2}$ (1,4-ndc) $\mathsf{\Gamma}_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{mdp})](\mathrm{H}_{2}0)\}$ , $\left[\mathsf{C d}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathrm{mdp})\right]$ , $\{\vert\mathsf{C d}_{2}(1,4\$ -ndc) $(1,4\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Hndc})_{2}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{mdp})_{2}](\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{0.5}\}$ $\begin{array}{r l}{[\operatorname{H}_{2}\mathrm{mdp}}&{{}=}\end{array}$ methylene bis(3,5- dimethylpyrazole)] are built from same colignad with different compositions. The first two MOFs were built upon paddle-wheel SBUs had trans geometry of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{mdp}$ , but the third one contains two different types of binuclear subunits formed by a pair of $\operatorname{Cd}(\operatorname{II})$ ions and it possessed partly deprotonated dicarboxylic acid with neutral coligand, had cis-conformation of $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathrm{mdp}$ . There are large numbers of cadmium based luminescent MOF, Cd-1,4-ndc MOF are no exceptions, for example, cadmium based MOF having binuclear SBUs based on $[\mathsf{C d}_{2}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathsf{b i b})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ showed luminescence [52b].
Macrocyclic complex $\{{\mathrm{Bu}}_{2}{\mathrm{Sn}}(1,4{\mathrm{-ndc}})\}_{4}{\cdot}2{\mathrm{C}}_{6}\mathrm{H}_{6}$ was found to have shown good antitumor activities [53a] The lead based MOFs, $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{P b}(1,4\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathsf{D M F})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ and $\mathsf{\{P b(1,4-n d c)(D M A)\}_{n}}$ showed scintillating signals under $\mathbf{x}.$ -rays, these signals were triggered by a highly purified tungsten-target [53b]; accordingly they showed the abilities to convert X-ray to normal light.
Zirconium based 1,4-ndc MOF UiO-66(AN) had shown photocatalytic activities [54a]. The changes in the structural aspects of the MOFs by adding another linker of similar kind is interest to to tune properties of the original MOF. In such studied carried out with 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylate based zirconium metal–organic framework [54b,54c] with UiO-66 topology (UiO abbreviation of University of Oslo) with different fractions of the 1,4- benzene dicarboxylic acid revealed decrease in the thermal stability of the parent MOF by such an introduction. However, using modulator structural properties of the final MOF could be tuned so as to increases crystal sizes as well as surface areas. Nitrogen absorption studies had revealed that MOF with only 1,4-ndc was the most efficient sample, but its performance declined at high pressures. However, performance trend at higher pressure improved with increasing amounts of 1,4-benzene dicarboxylate, reflecting the availability of larger internal pore volumes in the MOFs when it had a higher fraction of smaller linkers.
Lanthanide 1,4-ndc MOFs either as homo-metallic or heterometallic unit have their own characteristic features. For example, isostructural fcu-MOFs $[(\mathrm{CH_{3}})_{2}\mathrm{NH_{2}}]_{2}[\mathrm{M_{6}}(\upmu_{3}\mathrm{-OH})_{8}(1,4\mathrm{-}$ $\mathrm{ndc)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{6}]\cdot(s o l v e n t)_{\it x}}$ $(\mathsf{M}\ =\ \mathsf{E u}^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , and $\Upsilon^{3+}$ ) possesing 12- connected $\small\mathrm{\vert{M_{6}(\mu_{3}-O H)_{8}(-O_{2}C)_{12}}}$ SBUs were discovered by Eddaoudi and coworkers [55] In the Eu-MOF, the presence of the 12-connected Eu-based building units in combination with octahedral and tetrahedral cages resulted in the fcu-MOF. These units connected together provided smaller/contracted triangular window aperture to the Eu-MOF. These MOFs showed relatively higher ${C O}_{2}/{N}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{C H}_{4}$ selectivity. For example, between the two isomorphic mixed carboxylate homo-metallic MOFs [M(tca)(1,4-ndc) $\mathrm{\sfH}_{2}{\sf O}\mathrm{\largeI}_{\mathrm{n}}$ $\mathbf{M}=\mathbf{E}\mathbf{u}$ or Tb and $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{tca}=4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime}$ -tricarboxytriphenylamine) both had shown comparable emission decay life-time in milisecond range, but the quantum yield of terbium-MOF was found to be $11\%$ , whereas that of the europium –MOF was $0.06\%$ making a large difference in efficiencies of emissions [56]. Eu-MOF with mixed acid linkers of 4-(pyridin-3-yloxy)phthalic acid ( $\mathrm{\cdotH}_{2}$ ppda) and naphthalene-1,4-dicarboxylic acid had shown fluorescence emission over a wide $\mathsf{p H}$ range 2–13. It was found to be a useful analyte for detecting $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ , ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{A l}^{3+}$ ions as well as biologically important phosphate anion. Furthermore, it is also a sensor for trinitrophenol [57a] which is a potential explosive material. Water soluble hetero-metallic MOF $\mathrm{\Delta[Eu_{6}Z n(\mu_{3}-O H)_{8}(1,4-n d c)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{6}]_{n}}$ was highly specific to show changes in fluorescence response towards antibiotic ronidazole [57b].
## 3.3. 2,6-Naphthalenedicarboxylate MOFs
Among the different MOFs derived from the various positional isomers of naphthalenedicarboxylate, the 2,6-isomeric motifs are widely studied, and these are associated with various properties which are listed in the Table 3 and Table 4. The Table 3 has the examples of alkali metal, alkaline earth metal ions MOF and suggests the broad utilities of those. Carbon dioxide from hydrocarbon mixture was separated by zinc-2,6-ndc based MOF having N,N’-di(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide (dpni) coligand [22a]. Acid gases such as ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ , $S0_{2}$ , nitrogen oxides, and $\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{S}$ are removed by ndc based MOF of different metal ions having nitrogen based coligands [22b].
Metal organic framework of lithium-2,6-ndc of composition $\lvert\mathrm{Li}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})\rvert_{\mathrm{n}}$ was found to be thermally stable up to $600^{\circ}\mathsf C$ ; crystal structure of it shows layers of two-dimensional antifluorite structure [58]. Magnesium based MOF, $[\mathsf{M g}_{3}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}(\mathsf{D M F})_{4}]$ showed violet emission [59a] and is catalyst for three-component reactions between of aromatic aldehydes, malononitrile, and cyanoacetamide. Due to competition of solvent to coordinate it formed an ionic complex [59b] $\mathrm{\Delta[Mg(DMF)_{2}(H_{2}O)_{4}]cdot2,6}$ -ndc. While, magnesium 2,6-ndc MOF form $\mathsf{N{\mathrm{-}}G Q D s}/\mathsf{E u}^{3+}@\mathsf{M g\mathrm{-}M O F}$ (N$\mathtt{G Q D S}=\mathbb{N}$ atom doped graphene quantum dots and ${\bf\cal M}{\bf g-M O F=\Gamma}=$ $\{[\mathsf{M g}_{3}(2,6\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ndc})_{2.5}(\mathsf{H C O}_{2})_{2}(\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O})][\mathsf{N H}_{2}\mathsf{M e}_{2}]\cdot2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\cdot\mathsf{D M F}\}$ was highly photoluminiscent [59c]. These hybrid materials were useful to detect benzene and related aromatic compounds. Calcium MOF of 4,8-disulfonyl-2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylic acid (sulf2,6- $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{ndc})$ has PtS-type topology and it is a similar MOF as of the corresponding cadmium MOF with the same dicarboxylic acid. However, with copper(II) ions the sulf2,6-ndc formed onedimensional coordination polymer, that had high proton conductance of magnitude $3.46\times10^{-3}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $368\mathrm{~K~}$ at $95\%$ relative humidity (RH) [59d].
An aluminium based MOF known as CAU-3 (CAU $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ ChristianAlbrechts-Universitaet) $\mathrm{[Al_{2}(O C H_{3})_{4}(2,6-n d c)]}$ , possesses methoxide-bridges forming dodecameric SBUs. The $[\mathsf{A l}_{12}(-$ $\mathsf{O C H}_{3})_{24}]^{12+}$ SBUs led to a fcc-packing, similar to the one observed in UiO-66 [60a]. This MOF provided unusually high Langmuir surface area of $2750\mathrm{~A~m}^{2}\mathrm{~g}^{-1}$ . Nitro- or amino-substituted 2,6-ndc containing sulfone-bridged groups, were fund to have permanent porosity which could accommodate carbon dioxide or hydrogen or methane gas [60b]. Two aluminium based metal organic frameworks were synthesized using a solvothermal route. The structure of $[\mathsf{A l}(0\mathsf{H})(2,6\mathsf{-n}\mathsf{d}\mathsf{c})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ MOF had a structural backbone constituted by chains formed of 2,6-ndc ligands bridging aluminium ions. It has octahedral environment around the aluminium ion in each node [60c]. Once the solvent molecules were removed from the pristine MOF, it had $0.68\ \mathrm{cm}^{3}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ pore volume and it was also capable to retain hydrogen gas in slightly higher amounts than the overall pore volumes. This could have probably occurred due to the adsorptions of hydrogen gas on the outer surface of the MOF.
Single-crystal nanowires of the $A|^{3+}2,6$ -ndc MIL-69(Al) MOF by using graphene oxide Nano scrolls as structure-directing agents was established in literature [60d]. Preferential growth of the MIL-69(Al) Nano-wires along [001] crystallographic direction was achieved. The nanowires had an average diameter of $70\pm20~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and lengths up to $2~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ . These were formed due to size-confinement and templating effects. During the course of synthesis, the MIL-69(Al) seeds and self-scrolls of graphene-oxide sheets were initially formed. It was followed by the anisotropic growth of MIL-69(Al) crystals mediated by specific grapheneoxide sheets/MOF interactions.
N,N-Dimethylacetamide containing manganese MOFs, namely, $\mathsf{[M n_{3}(2,6-n d c)_{3}(D M A)_{4}]_{n}}$ and $\begin{array}{r l}{{[}\mathrm{Mn}_{1.5}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{DMA})_{2}]}&{{}}\end{array}$ are of general interest [61a,61b]. The latter MOF underwent reversible structural transformation upon removal and retake of DMA molecules. It is also a precursor for manganous oxide nanocrystals of ${\sim}10-$ $18~\mathrm{{nm}}$ embedded in a carbonaceous layer [61b]. Iron based 2,6- ndc MOF, [MIL 88C] with a composition $[\mathrm{Fe}_{3}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}\mathrm{X}]$ $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{\langle{X}={O H}}$ , Cl)] has a pore dimension $4030\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ ; it is a good catalyst for rearrangement of $\textsf{\textsf{a}}$ -pinene oxide to camphonelal and isopinocamphone under solvent free conditions [61].
The MOF DUT-8(Ni) (DUT - Dresden University of Technology) with a building unit $[\mathsf{N i}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}\mathsf{d a b c o l}$ opens its pores upon exposure to polar aprotic solvents; but in alcohols it does opening of pores but by doing so it also maintains a tight-packed structure [62a]. Host-guest interactions were suggested as the cause of such differences in pore-opening; these aspects were also reflected in step-like switching in absorptions of substrate shown by the MOF at a characteristic threshold concentration during gas absorption studies. Iron based mixed carboxylate $[\mathrm{Fe}_{4}(1,4{\-}\mathrm{bdc})_{2}(2,6{\-}\mathrm{ndc})$ 1 $(\mathsf{S O_{4}})_{4}(\mathsf{d m a m})_{4}]$ (1,4-bdc $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ benzene-1,4-dicarboxylate; dmam $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ dimethyl ammonium) showed high proton conductivity $2.90\times10^{-2}\mathrm{~S~cm}^{-1}$ at $95^{\circ}$ and $60\%$ relative humidity [62b]. Use of sulphonated 2,6-ndc in nickel MOF called as DUT- $4{\cdot}S O_{3}\mathrm{H}$ is a catalyst [62c] for the nucleophilic ring opening reactions of styrene oxide by methanol. For switchable intake and release of gas it is essential for selective separation of adsorbed gases. Due to differences in type of nodes the MOFs $[\mathrm{M}_{2}(2,6{\-}\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{d}{\mathrm{abc}}0)]$ known as DUT8( $\mathbf{\dot{M}}=C\mathbf{0}$ or Ni) showed distinct differences [62d] in gating adsorption behaviour of adsorption of nitrogen gas. These two MOF showed closed pores after removal of solvents; however, DUT8(Ni) showed better gate opening in for nitrogen adsorption, $\mathsf{p}/\mathsf{p}_{0}=0.1$ (77 K). This caused large change in pore volume, but the DUT8(Co) did not absorb nitrogen at $77~\mathrm{{K}}$ by maintaining its pores closed. These aspects were studied through in-situ electron spin resonance spectroscopy.
Resin-assisted synthesis have provided different cobalt-based MOFs that are conventionally difficult to synthesise such as $[\mathsf{C o}_{3}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}(\mathsf{D M F})_{4}]$ , $\mathsf{\Gamma}[\mathsf{C o}_{3}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}(\mathsf{M e}_{2}\mathsf{N H})_{2}]$ , and [Co (2,6-ndc)(DMF)] [63a]. Variations in the nodes by coligands have enabled to obtain different compositions; for example $\ C_{0_{3}}(2,6-$ n $\mathrm{{dc})_{3}(b b i)(M e C N)_{2}}$ , [bbi $\begin{array}{r l}{\mathbf{\Phi}}&{{}=\ \mathbf{1},\mathbf{1}^{\prime}.}\end{array}$ -(1,4-butanediyl)bis(imidazole)], had self-penetrating network [63b], whereas 2-fold interpenetrated $\{[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-}\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathsf{b p e e})_{2}](\mathsf{b p e e})\}$ $(\mathrm{bpee}=4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridineethy lene) [63c] absorbed selectively $40.5~\mathrm{mLg}^{-1}$ carbon dioxide or 29 $\mathrm{m}\mathrm{Lg}^{-1}$ of benzene; it did not adsorb nitrogen gas [63c]. It was also found in the magnetic susceptibility measurement that the desolvated MOF had shown a square hysteresis loop. The MOF NU-1000, had hydroxide/aqua ligands with overall had 8- connected $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}$ nodes $Z\mathrm{r}_{6}(\upmu^{3}-\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\upmu^{3}-\mathrm{O})_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{4}$ nodes and had variety of metal precursors occured in the small cavities within the MOF. The 2,6-ndc ligands were used to bridge between two adjacent nodes in NU-1000 and was incorporated in a ratio of 1 to 2.6 numbers of 2,6-ndc linkers per node [63d]. These permitted cobalt ions to coordinate in a relatively unfavorable manner from the initial coordination in zirconium pyrenetetrayltetrakis(benzoate). Hence, a direct control over structure to find out locations of the catalytic species deposited on amorphous supports could be done as it could be possible to block the small cavities present in the original MOF. It was found that having cobalt oxide located at different positions of the MOF, the resulting material retained catalytic activity as oxidative dehydrogenation catalyst at low temperature. This observation had supported that the utility of the supports of the crystals as a required parameter for the efficient catalytic process.
Neon gas trapping by metal–organic framework $|\mathsf{C o}_{3}(2,6-$ $\mathrm{ndc})_{3}(\mathrm{dabco})]$ was performed over a wide range of temperatures and pressures. An amount of $6.1\mathrm{mol}/\mathrm{m}^{3}$ of neon could be delivered at 1 bar pressure at $300~\mathrm{K}$ by increasing gas pressure to 65 bar at $150~\mathrm{K}$ by using the MOF [63e]. However, an enhanced amount of neon gas $(18.5~\mathrm{\mol/m}^{3})$ could be delivered with the same discharge conditions once storage temperature was decreased to $77~\mathrm{K}.$ This demonstrated utility of using ndc MOF for storage of an inert gas. The storage of helium and other inert gases are of general interest and is a challenge, where the intrinsic behaviour of trapping is yet to be understood fully.
Single-crystal to single-crystal transformations of a highly porous doubly-interpenetrated framework $[\mathsf{C o}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}(\mathsf{b p y})]$ to a less porous but triply-interpenetrated framework through an intermediate empty doubly-interpenetrated was observed [6]. The molecular process involving structural changes in the course of transformationsis shown in Fig. 14. The Fig. 14(a) represents the pristine form of the structures of single crystal of the MOF having DMF guest molecules; at $80^{\circ}C$ it reversibly desorbs or upon cooling DMF environment it absorbs DMF molecules to form a metastable crystal without losing crystallinity. The metastable form transforms to an MOF with empty pores (Fig. 14b). This metastable form upon heating to $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ provided a triply interpenetrated structure shown in Fig. 14c; alternatively, the pristine form on heating at $120^{\circ}\mathsf C$ formed the triply penetrating form. Since DMF molecules have low molecular weight with respect to the overall composition of the MOF, the crystal volumes were not significantly affected upon the loss of DMF, hence the crystal to crystal transformation were possible.
Several studied were based on 1,4-diazabicyclo[2.2.2]-octane (dabco) containing Ni-2,6-ndc MOF $\mathrm{[Ni_{2}(2,6-n d c)_{2}d a b c o]_{n}}$ were carried out [64a, 64b,64c, 64d, 64e, 64f]. It was known from these studies that MOF DUT-8(Ni), DUT $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ Dresden University of Technology, provided scopes to study in-situ study gate opening mechanism while gas absorption and desorption were taking place. An increased in volume change of unit cell of this MOF DUT-8(Ni) up to $254\%$ was possible by adsorption of gases. The opening and closing of pores was also possible with this MOF. EXAFS study during ${\sf N}_{2}$ adsorption suggested the changes in local coordination geometry of the metal atoms during the structural transformation in flexible MOFs was due to deformation of paddle-wheel binuclear nickel carboxylate nodes was due to deformation of paddle-wheel binuclear. Xenon adsorption was also studied by using $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR spectroscopy; such study helped to monitor the ‘‘gate-pressure” effect. The sharpness of the $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR signal and chemical shifts were dependent on the structural transition from the low porosity state with narrow pores to a state having wide pores [64c]. In another study, it was found that the switching characteristics of adsorptions were dependent on the changes of crystal sizes. From the crystals having lower size than ${\sim}500~\mathrm{nm}$ , typical isotherm of rigid MOFs was observed. When the crystal sizes were higher than $1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ , they showed gating behaviour in gas adsorptions. Based on the observations from such studies a mono-domain switching process was revealed [64d]. The type of vibrational signals in tera-hertz observed during the gas absorption by the ‘‘rigid’’ and ‘‘flexible’’ forms of DUT-8(Ni) were analysed by tera-hertz spectroscopy and also with the support of the Raman spectroscopy [64e]. From a theoretical study it was suggested that the breathing modes of structures with the crystals with shorter diagon, seven periods of about 12.3 picoseconds were observed, these corresponded to 0.57THz $(\mathsf{\small{l}}8\mathrm{\cm}^{-1}),$ . However, for the crystals having long diagon there were ten periods of about 6 picoseconds corresponding to $1.5~\mathrm{THz}$ $\sim(50\ c m^{-1}),$ (Fig. 15). Hence signals for the two forms could be differentiated. The breathing modes for open gate and the close pore phases appeared in Raman spectroscopy at $23~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $60~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . These results complemented the theory and experimental descriptions on the controlled gating process. Related studies on the gas adsorption properties also suggested adsorption induced phase transition by this Ni-MOF [3].
On-chip conversion of $\mathsf{N i}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ layers to form 2,6-ndc based nickel MOF provided Ni-MOF film which did not undergo delamination upon activation by solvent soaking, nitrogen blowing for a limited time and in scotch-tape test [64e]. Hence, there are avenues to integrate such films onto microelectronic devices. Nano sheets of copper-2,6-ndc are biosensors for detection of DNA [65a].
On the other hand, reversible interconversion by transformation of trinuclear building blocks of copper(I) $\vert\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathsf{P y C})_{3}\vert$ to copper(II) $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathrm{II}}(\upmu\mathrm{-}\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{H P y}\mathsf{C})_{3}]^{2+}$ of a 2,6-ndc Cu MOF called as FDM-7 which possesses 4-pyrazolecarboxylate (PyC) coligand (Fig. 16a) was established. It was possible under oxidative conditions of the copper(I) MOF [65b]. A segment of the structure of the FDM-7 MOF is shown in Fig. 16b. The voids present in the MOF are shown in Fig. 13c. The reversible redox transformations between $\vert\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathsf{P y C})_{3}\vert$ and $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathrm{{II}}}(\upmu\mathrm{{-}}\mathsf{O H})(\mathsf{P y}\mathsf{C})_{3}(\mathsf{O H})_{3}]$ occurred without changing the original connectivity of the connecting geometries of the units as illustrated in the Fig. 16c. It was found that the linearcoordinated $\mathtt{C u(I)}$ ions in $\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathrm{{I}}}(\mathsf{P y C})_{3}$ got oxidized by $0_{2}$ in the presence water and such process played an active part in catalytic oxidation reactions of benzylic alcohols. The 2,6-ndc-Cu MOF possessing $[\mathsf{C u}_{3}^{\mathrm{I}}(\mathsf{H P y C})_{3}]$ linkers had three different types of cages with different shapes having pore dimensions $7.2\mathrm{~\AA~}\times7.2\mathrm{~\AA};$ 5.2 Å ${\bf\Phi}\times~11.2~\mathrm{\AA},$ $18.4\mathrm{~\AA~}\times21.6\mathrm{~\AA~}$ respectively (Fig. 16c). Different types pores were observed in the MOF as a consequence of different binding affinities of pyrazolate and carboxylate to copper ion as illustrated in the Fig. 16d by the equilibrium between two types of nodes formed by reversible bindings with hydroxyl group mediated by oxygen.
The zinc 2,6-ndc MOF are the largest family among all the MOFs of ndc with other metal ions and the fundamental MOF with solvent N,N-diethylformamide (DEF), $\mathrm{[Zn_{3}(O H)_{2}(2,6-n d c)_{2}]}.4\mathrm{DEF}.2\mathrm{H}_{2}-$ O. It has a three-dimensional structure derived from Zn-O-C secondary building units and it is devoid of catenation [66a]. Reticular synthesis by using 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene together with 2,6-ndc provided robust MOF-205 with high porosity (Fig. 17) is a very highly important and popular MOF. It showed reversible guest binding with aromatic guests [1g]. Studies on $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{2}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]$ (solvent)x $\mathbf{\epsilon}_{\mathbf{X}}=1.6$ for DMF, $\textbf{\em X}=1$ for benzene, toluene or $p$ -xylene) were isolated and structures were established. It was shown that p-xylene was preferentially separated from a mixture of isomers using the MOF. Deuterium
NMR was used establish the motions of deuterated guest molecules within the host; which showed that benzene underwent rapid hopping around its $C_{6}$ axis with an activation energy of $6~\mathrm{kJ~mol}^{-1}$ whereas the toluene executed rapid rotation of the $\mathsf{-C D}_{3}$ group [66b].
Cationic MOF of zinc $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{NEt}_{2})[\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\$ (acetate) $(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}]$ DEF possess paddlewheel SBUs [67a]; it has $13\mathrm{~\AA~}\times13$ Å square channels, which accommodate diethylformamide (DEF) molecules. The cationic part was formed during course of synthesis of the MOF in DEF, where a fraction of DEF molecules got hydrolysed. Upon removal of the DEF from the MOF the architecture collapsed.
However, cationic MOF $\mathrm{[H_{2}N(C H_{3})_{2}][Z n(2,6-n d c)(a t z)]\cdot H_{2}O}$ 1 ${\bf\dot{H}a t}z=$ 1H-tetrazol-5-amine) [67b] was found to be stable and was capable of selective adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ . It also showed fluorescence sensing responses upon addition of nitrobenzene.
It is a well-known fact that zinc and cadmium carboxylates adopt different structures depending on substituents, and reaction conditions [68]. It is also very much true in case of MOF and plays a key role in observing a SBU. There are number of SBU of zinc where besides carboxylate aqua and oxide bridges are found in conjuncture. For example, oxide-bridges in conjunction with carboxylate bridges of zinc-2,6-ndc MOF were found in mixed acid carboxylate MOFs. For example, robust framework of zinc carboxylate of triprotic acid 1,3,5-tris(4-carboxyphenyl)benzene (H3btb) (Fig. 14) together with 2,6-ndc linkers DUT-6, possessing N,Ndiethylformamide molecules was observed [69a]. Whereas, the MOFs of the same kind but the one that are devoid of solvent are known as MOF-205 [69b]. Those MOFs had $Z{\mit\mathrm{n}}_{4}0({\mathrm{CO}}_{2})_{6}$ SBUs and large size of SBU together with the large size of btb linkers; which provided high pore dimensions. The DUT-6 accommodates high amounts of methane gas $(230~\mathrm{\mg/g)}$ or hydrogen gas $(60~\mathrm{mg/g)}$ . The MOF-205 has $85\%$ void volume and bulk density is $0.38~\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{cm}^{3}$ , it showed $123~\mathrm{mg/g}$ uptake of hydrogen gas.
Aqua-bridges present in a SBU together with carboxylates linkers [70a] were found in $\mathrm{\small{[Zn(H_{2}O)_{2}(2,6-n d c)(n d i)]\cdot2H_{2}O}}$ (ndi $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ naphthalene carbodiimide); this MOF was photochromic. It showed photo-quenching upon irradiation by a 300 W xenon lamp; which photo-degradation was accompanied by formation of ndi radicals. Another water coordinated Zn-MOF, namely $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{tb}\mathrm{ad})(2,6-$ ndc) $_2(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]$ 1.5DMF $2\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ [tbad $\mathbf{\Psi}=\mathsf{N}\mathsf{1}$ -(4-(1H-1,2,4triazole-1-yl)be nzyl)- $\mathbf{\cdotN}^{1}$ -(2-aminoethyl)ethane-1,2-diamine] [70b], had $\boldsymbol{-}\mathsf{N}\mathsf{H}_{2}$ groups; it also had coordination unsaturation. These aspects together with the space provided by the MOF made rooms for ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ inclusion. It showed not only high selectivity for adsorption of ${\mathsf{C O}}_{2}$ over $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and coordination unsaturation allowed metal ion to activate carbon dioxide, hence it was identified as an efficient catalyst for insertion of carbon dioxide to epoxides forming cyclic carbonates under mild catalytic conditions. The DMF coordinated $Z_{\mathrm{{n-}}2,6}$ -ndc MOF was thermally decomposed to form zinc-oxide [70c]. Thin-film of this $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}0(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}]$ node containing MOF had a layer-like arrangement of the ndc units [70d]. It is a semiconductor with conductance $3.98\times10^{-2}\ \mathrm{S/cm}$ . In order to store methane gas in an MOF, it should have relatively small pores with diameter of about $0.40.8~\mathrm{{\nm}}$ . When a MOF possesses relatively large substituents in a linker not directly participating in coordination with the metal, a protion of such a linker may act as pendent group. When such pendent groups orient towards interior sides of the pores, those MOFs have partially or fully filled pores. These can effectively reduce the spaces in the tight packed arrangements of the MOFs. These effects decided the hydrophilicity or hydrophobicity of a pore; in the event of reductions of pore sizes, more quantity of methane gas could be retained. Accordingly, the $\mathtt{Z n_{4}O}$ metal clusters with 2,6-ndc having tert-butylethynyl or phenylethynyl groups on the ring showed a higher uptake of methane gas [70e] as compared to the one having higher pore diameters.
Coligands of zinc-2,6-ndc MOFs play major roles to dictate the sizes, shapes, voids and providing directional properties to MOFs and also the coligands influence the properties of zinc-2,6-ndcMOFs possessing binuclear zinc-paddle wheel nodes. For example, the MOF with coligand N,N’-di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenete tracarboxydiimide (dipyni) connected to $[Z\mathsf{n}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}]$ was capable of interacting with lithium cations. Upon doping the MOF with lithium ions, it showed selective adsorption of carbon dioxide over hydrogen and methane gas [71a]. 2,6-Naphthalenedicarboxylate zinc MOF having bidentate bridging coligands are common. For example the $\mathrm{\Gamma[Zn(2,6-ndc)(4-bpmh)]_{n}}$ , (4-bpmh $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ N,N-bis-pyridin
4-ylmethylene-hydrazine) [71b], $\mathrm{Zn_{2}(2,6-n d c)_{2}(d n p i)}$ [dnpi ${\bf\Lambda}={\bf N},{\bf N}^{\prime}$ -di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide] [71c], $[\mathrm{Zn}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{0.5}(\mathrm{bpa})_{0.5}(\mathrm{SO}_{4})\cdot(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}\mathrm{NH}],$ , (bpa $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ bis-pyridin-4-yla mide) [71d] $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{tpom})(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}]\cdot3\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ [tpom $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ tetrakis(4-pyri dyloxymethyl)methane] [71e], $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(2,6-n d c)_{2}(d p n i)]}$ [dpni $\mathrm{\Lambda=N,N^{\prime}-}$ di-(4-pyridyl)-1,4,5,8-naphthalenetetracarboxydiimide] [71f], were structurally established and each had their own merit on gas absorptions capabilities. Most commonly adsorption studies were carried out with carbon dioxide and methane and each MOF showed limited absorption capabilities. The photoluminescent MOF, $[Z\mathrm{n}_{2}(\mathrm{tpom})(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}]\cdot3\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was suitable for selective detection of $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ and ${\mathsf{C r}}^{6+}$ ions [71e].
Density differences among MOFs provided means for rapid purification of certain concomitantly formed MOFs. The MOF, $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(2,6-n d c)(d i q u n i)]_{n}}}$ (diquni $\mathbf{\Lambda}=\mathbf{N},\mathbf{N}^{\prime}$ -bis(quinolin-5-yl)-1,4,5,8-n aphthaldiimide) was purified by this concept as one phase of this MOF floated in $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{BrCl}$ , while the other phase sunk [72]. On the other hand, post synthetic modifications of MOF [Zn(2,6-ndc) $(4,4^{\prime}{\-}\mathsf{-b p e})_{0.5}]_{\mathrm{n}}$ $[4,4^{\prime}\mathrm{-bpe~=~}4,4^{\prime}$ -trans-bis(4-pyridyl)ethylene] was achieved by incorporation of silver ion to the framework. The silver ions interacted with the framework of the MOF through $\pi$ -bonds. This was a reason that this MOF had higher ability to adsorb olefins over paraffin [73]. Exchange of small guest molecules with minimal volume change of a unit-cell of a crystal facilitates a single crystal to single crystal transformation. Such a single-crystal to single-crystal transformation was observed in $[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}(\mathrm{DMF})$ $(\mathrm{ur})_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}]$ $\mathbf{\dot{u}r=}$ hexamethylenetetramine also known as urotropin) [74a]. This zinc-MOF was observed as a good host for benzene or ferrocene. The photoluminescence of the MOF was largely influenced by interactions with guest molecules. In yet another hexamethylenetetramine based MOF $[Z\mathrm{n}_{4}(\mathrm{ur})_{2}(\mathrm{ndc})_{4}]$ possesses pores of two different sizes. It showed step by step activation of the bi-porous structure with labile $\mathsf{S_{4}N_{4}}$ molecules [74b]. Due to the difference in environments of the pores of the MOF, it was also possible to separate individual components of benzene and $\mathsf{S}_{4}\mathsf{N}_{4}$ from a mixture of these two compounds. The MOF named as CAU-5, $\mathrm{|Zn_{2}(2,6-n d c)_{2}(a p b)|_{n}}$ ( $\mathsf{a b p}=3$ -azo-phenyl-4,40bipyridin e) has a 2-fold interpenetrated structure. The coligand having azo-functional groups protruding into the pores had a role in structural changes caused by stimuli. Upon photo-irradiation at $360\ \mathrm{nm}$ of the MOF having trans geometry on the azo-groups, underwent trans to cis isomerisation, whereas the cis form of the MOF was transformed to the trans form by irradiation at $440~\mathrm{{nm}}$ [74c]. The 2,6-ndc MOFs of bivalent zinc, cobalt and cadmium each possessing $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -dipyridyl disulphide (aldrithiol) as coligand, were found to be compositionally and structurally wide apart. The cobalt MOF was useful in iodine absorption, whereas the zinc and cadmium counterparts had shown proton conductivities with magnitude within the range of semi-conductors [74d]. Bischelating co-ligand 1,10-phenanthroline containing Zn-1,6-ndc MOFs $\{[\mathrm{Zn}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathrm{phen})]\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ (phen $\mathit{\Theta}=\ 1{,}10$ -phenanthroline) is a sensor for electron-donating aromatic amine guests [75a]. Aromatic amines cause enhancement of intensity fluorescence emission of the MOF. On the other hand, $\mathrm{\small{[Zn_{2}(i p h e n)_{2}(2,6-n d c)_{2}]_{n}}}$ (iphen $\mathbf{\Psi}=1$ -H-imidazo[4,5-f][1,10]-phenanthroline) [75b], showed red-shifted emission with respect to the emission shown by the parent iphen. Though co-ligand has additional binding sites from the imidazole unit, those sites remained free and the 2,6-ndc ligands adopt a $\upmu^{2}$ -bridging modes. Zig-zag chains with terminal iphen ligands were present at the either side of the chains. those constituted the back-bone suitable for the formation of a supramolecular assembly.
In a similar manner the properties and special featurers of 2,6- ndc MOFs of second and third row elements as well as lanthanides are well documented and they also have found extensive attentions in the field of reticular chemistry. Some of the 2,6-ndc MOFs of heavy-metal ions are listed in Table 4. Cadmimum MOF having secondary building units of binuclear carboxylates interlinked by1,3-bis(4-pyridyl)propane (bpp) provided MOF ${\mathsf{I C d}}_{2}(2,6-$ ndc)2(bpp)(DMF)] 2DMF formed from a reaction carried out in dimethylformamide [76a]. This MOF was a six connected interpenetrating one. Incorporation of pyrrole as a redox-active co-ligand to the one dimensional nano-channels of the $[\mathsf{C d}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{0.5}(\mathsf{p c a})]$ (pca $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ pyridine carboxylate) was observed [76b]. This pyrrole guest included MOF upon immersing into iodine, there was a 9- fold increase of the bulk electrical conductance of the MOF. The increase was due to formation of highly directional oriented polypyrrole inside the channels of the MOF. In certain cases, the solvent coordinated and without solvent crystallised out in concomitant way. The crystal sizes were of large difference and these allowed them to separate by hand picking from the mixture of crystals. Such an example is the crystals of the [Cd (2,6-ndc)(DMF)] and $[\mathsf{C d}_{3}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}]$ formed together in a reaction carried out in DMF, these two MOFs could be separated from the solution from where it crystallised out by picking up crystals of the different morphologies from solution [76c]. These two MOFs were found to easily thermally decompose, hence acted as precursor for cadmium nano-particles. Cationic indium 2,6-ndc MOF, $\mathrm{(Et_{2}N H_{2})}[\mathrm{In}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{2}]\cdot2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\cdot\mathrm{I}$ DEF poseses three distinct types of pores [77]. It showed temperature dependent uptake of carbon dioxide, $13.3\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $196\mathrm{K},3.22\mathrm{mmol}/\AA$ $\slash\mathbf{g}$ at $273\ \mathrm{K},$ and $1.78\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ .
Lanthanide base MOF are of interest in opto-electronics, sensors and in showing interesting magnetic properties. Luminescence thermometer properties were observed in lanthanide based MOF that were formed with different compositions based on the central metal ion. Depending on composition structures differed in Labased 2,6-nd MOFs and these were categorised in three categories based on compositions such as $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathrm{LnCl}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})(\mathrm{DMF})]$ for $\ln^{3+}\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ ${\mathrm{La}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{C e}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{P r}^{3^{-}}$ , ${\mathsf{N}}{\mathsf{d}}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}^{3+}$ , $[\mathrm{Eu}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{DMF})]{\cdot}0.5\mathrm{DM}$ F, and $[\mathrm{Ln}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]$ for $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}=\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ , $\mathsf{D y}^{3+}$ , $\Upsilon^{3+}$ , $\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ , $\Upsilon\mathsf{b}^{3+}$ [78a]. Trinuclear cluster $[\mathrm{Ln}_{3}(\upmu^{3}-\mathrm{OH})(2,6-\mathrm{ndc})_{3}(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})_{6}]\cdot3\mathrm{DMF}\cdot5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O};$ $\operatorname{Ln}=\operatorname{Tb}$ , Eu, Dy, Er, Yb, Gd was identified as a fluorescent probe to detect $\mathsf{F e}^{3+}$ selectively [78b] Fluorescence sensing of ${\mathsf{C r}}^{3+}$ ion in concentration range $1\times10^{-3}$ to $7.5\times10^{-5}\mathrm{mol/L}$ was observed with a zinc metal-org. framework with ${\mathsf{E u}}^{3+}$ ions [78c]. Mixed lanthanide MOF, $\mathrm{[Eu_{3.2}G d_{0.8}(2,6-n d c)_{6}(H_{2}O)_{5}]}{\cdot3}\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ showed dual fluorescence emissions [78d]. Due to response to change emission of the MOF by formaldehyde, it was used to detect formaldehyde in the range $0.05{-}2.5\%$ . Detection of contaminants in food is a concern from health as economical point of view. To detect food contaminations, there are certain contaminants that are formed during biological conditions, one such contaminant is 3-nitropropionic acid; which is a secondary metabolite. It could be detected at low concentration by using an Eu-MOF constructed with 2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylate linker. The MOF was a dual emitter [78e]. The emissions of the MOF could be switched by changing pH. It was shown that MOF discrete trinuclear europium clusters with 2,6-ndc linkers undergo crystal to crystal transformation accompanied by luminescence switching. It was also observed that $\mathsf{p H}$ in the range of 4.00 to 3.00; there was increase in the bond length of $\mathtt{E u\mathrm{-}O_{\mathrm{ndc}}}$ accompanied by a decrease in ${\mathrm{Eu}}{-}0_{\mathrm{water}}$ bond. This hindered the energy transfer process from ligands to Eu ions and stood as a barrier for the ‘‘antenna effect’’ of the MOF at this pH. Hence red emission observed under ordinary condition was changed to purple emission; and finally upon change of pH it turned to blue fluorescence. These changes enabled to detect 3- nitropropionic acid by the MOF. The thorium metal–organic frameworks of 1,4- or 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate are known as ThSINAP-7 and Th-SINAP-8 respectively. These MOFs were found to be stable in $\mathsf{p H}1$ to 12 [78f]. Whereas, they also were thermally stable up to $520^{\circ}{\mathsf C}$ and they were not affected by ionizing radio resistance of $2\times10^{5}$ Gy of $\upbeta$ and $\gamma$ radiations. Among the two MOFs the ThSINAP-8 removed $>99\%$ iodine from a cyclohexane solution and had the capability to uptake $473\mathrm{\mg/g}$ of iodine vapour.
Depending on the coordinating solvents dimethyl acetamide, dimethyl formamide or water in the coordination sphere, different coordination polymers of neodymium were reported in literature, namely $[\mathrm{Nd}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{DMF})_{2}]\cdot\mathrm{DMF}\cdot\mathrm{CH}_{3}0\mathrm{H}\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ [Nd(2,6- ndc) $_{1.5}(\mathrm{DMA})_{0.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)]$ 1.5DMA, and $\mathrm{[Nd}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})_{1.5}(\mathrm{H}_{2}0)_{2}]{\cdot}2\mathrm{DM}f$ . Each solvent-free framework had differences in BET surface areas [79a]. Optimum conditions for synthesis La-2,6-ndc MOFs by varying different parameters were studied and observed that molar ratio laththanun salt to 2,6-ndc in 1:1 to 1.5 provided crystalline MOF. The adsorption/desorption isotherms of ${\sf N}_{2}$ suggested that the MOF had a surface area $270.4~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ [79b]. The lanthanum 2,6-ndc MOF were reported to include coordinated ethylene urea ligands in their open channels [79c]. Mixed metallic coordination polymer $\mathrm{\small{[Dy_{2}M n_{3}(2,6-n d c)_{6}(b b i)_{2}]}}$ , $[\mathsf{b}\mathsf{b}\mathsf{i}=1,1^{\prime}$ -(1,4-butanediyl)bis( imidazole)] is comprised of ${\tt D y}_{2}{\tt M n}_{3}$ cluster constituting an 8- connected node [63b]. It showed dominant ferromagnetic coupling interactions between $\mathsf{D y}^{3+}$ and $\mathsf{M n}^{2+}$ ions.
There are examples of using a substrate guiding the structure and composition of a final MOF without participating as part of the crystalline product. Their role is to guide the crystallisation. One such example is the lead(II) metal organic framework $[\mathrm{Pb}_{2}(\upmu^{4}-0)$ (bdc)DMF] having channel-like structure and $\mathsf{P b}(2,6-$ ndc)(DMF) having rectangular grid-like frameworks. These were crystallised by using $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -azopyridine for the former one and pyrazine for latter one as promoters in their respective reaction mixtures used in synthesis [80a]. Both the MOFs showed moderate uptake of nitrogen and hydrogen gases. Solvent free halide containing lead-MOF $([\mathsf{P b}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})1.5(\upmu^{2}\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C l})]_{\mathrm{n}})$ and Pb-SMOF-Br $([\mathsf{P b}_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})1.5(\upmu^{3}\mathrm{-Br})]_{\mathrm{n}})$ were found to heat resistant [80b]. These MOFs were better X-ray absorbers and were better in detection of $\mathsf{X}$ -ray doses than usually used $\mathsf{C s P b B r}_{3}$ for such a detection and has promise in hazard managements. Due to the inter-ligand charge transfer emission these MOFs were phosphorescent at room temperature. 2,6-Bis(pyrazin-2-ylthio)pyr azine (bptp) containing MOF possessing argentophilic silverdimers were observed in $\{[\mathsf{A g}_{2}(\mathsf{b p t p})_{2}(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})]{\cdot}3\mathsf{H}_{2}0\}_{\mathrm{n}}$ which has an overall slab-like architecture [80c]. Argentophilic interactions were suggested to provide additional stability to 1Dinfinite cationic chains of silver-ligand units in $[\mathsf{A g}_{2}(4,\mathsf{\hat{4}-b p y})_{2}]$ (2,6-ndc) $\boldsymbol{\cdot}\boldsymbol{4}\mathrm{H}_{2}\boldsymbol{0}$ [80d]. This coordination polymer was a photocatalyst for decolourization of dye by UV-irradiation.
Zirconium based 2,6-ndc have wide varieties of structural variations and material properties. The photoluminescence study on $Z\mathrm{r}{-}2{,}6$ -ndc MOF namely $\mathrm{[Zr_{6}O_{4}(O H)_{4}(2,6-n d c)_{12}]}$ had high emission quantum yield $(30\%)$ This high emitting state was attributed to a ligand-to-cluster charge-transfer process with the subsequent formation of a long-lived charge-separated state in the MOFs [81a]. The MOF when encapsulated fluorescent dyes such as coumarin 153, or 4-(dicyanomethylene)-2-methyl-6-(4-dimethylaminos tyryl)-4H-pyran, into the MOF pores they show OLED properties. In view of an energy transfer between the MOF and the trapped dye molecules, these dye incorporations helped to tune the electroluminescence properties of the parent MOF and provided a means to develop smart MOFs as electroactive layers for LEDs. In quest for light harvesting and photonic smart-materials photo-behaviour of a Zr-di-methyl 2,6-ndc was studied. Composite material of the MOF was prepared by embedding coumarin153@Zr-2,6-ndc, within a polymeric membrane of poly-bisphenol-A carbonate-co$^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -(3,3,5-trimethylcyclohexylidene)diphenolcarbonate] [81b]. This mixed matrix membrane (MMM) exhibited inter-particle excimer-like emission with a red-shifted broad emission band. An energy transfer process from the excited MOF to the trapped coumarin molecule was observed. MMM has $25\%$ fluorescence quantum yield that well sought for preparation of light emitting diode based devices from composite materials. A photoluminescent zirconium metal-org. framework DUT-52 having an optical energy band-gap energy of $4.32\ \mathrm{eV}$ was synthesized from reaction of zirconium tetrachloride and 2,6- naphthalenedicarboxylic acid in DMF under microwave heating suggested that longer time period required for a same synthesis could be reduced by microwave heating [82a]. Practical utilities of a material in the form of thin films as membranes for separation and sensing have attracted attentions. Atomic or molecular layer deposition (ALD/MLD) techniques for thin film was improvised with 2,6-ndc linkers [82b]. The film deposited using $\mathrm{\Delta}Z\mathrm{r}{\mathrm{Cl}_{4}}$ together with $2,6\mathrm{-H_{2}n d c}$ was amorphous but transformed to crystalline MOF upon heating in acetic acid vapour environment. The film was luminescent and had shown antibacterial properties suggesting a possible practical utility.
Depending on modulator concentrations various $Z\mathrm{r}$ -and Hfbased MOFs DUT MOFs (DUT $\mathbf{\sigma}=\mathbf{\sigma}$ Dresden University of Technology) [DUT-84 $\left[{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{\mathit{Z}}}}\mathbf{\boldsymbol{\mathbf{r}}}\right)$ , DUT-53(Hf), DUT-52(Zr), andDUT-52(Hf)] with different topologies were prepared [82c]. Hence, it was possible to prepare SBUs with increased connectivity from six to eight and to twelve. It has been emphasized that modulator concentration plays major role in stabilizing the $\left[Z\mathrm{r}_{6}0_{4}(\mathrm{OH})_{4}\right]$ and $\left[\mathrm{Hf}_{6}0_{6}(\mathrm{OH})_{2}\right]$ clusters in these MOFs, in absence of controlled amount of modulator formation of amorphous precipitates took place. MOFs of the 12- connected DUT- ${\cdot}52(\mathrm{Zr})$ have higher catalytic activity for carbon dioxide activation than the corresponding MOFs having six- or eight- connected SBUs to prepare $\gamma$ -valerolactone. The density functional theory was applied to understand the reaction mechanism of the reaction DUT-52(Zr)-catalysed cycloaddition reactions of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ .
The MOFs Zr-2,6ndc/tetrazole and Zr-2,6-ndc/carbonitrile are fluorescent due to excimers formed interactions between neighboring naphthalene linkers. The emission of the $Z\mathrm{r}{-}2,6{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{n}\mathrm{d}\mathrm{c}/$ tetrazole was quenched upon interactions various nitro-benzene derivatives [82d]. The fluorescence of Zr-2,6-ndc/tetrazole is highly quenched by tri-nitro phenol which was attributed to hydrogen bonding and subsequent charge-transfer complex which was confirmed from the observed red-shifts. A short life time in emission life-time decay profile was also observed from sample the sample of MOF interacting with tri-nitro phenol. Similar quenching was observed with the Zr-2,6-ndc/carbonitrile MOF also. Intra-particle excimer formation between neighbouring naphthalene motifs of $Z\mathrm{r}{-}2{,}6$ -ndc MOF in different solution at low concentrations was reflected in a red-shifted broad band in the emission spectrum [82e]. The emission had three components with life-times $\tau_{1}=650$ ps, $\tau_{2}=3.7$ ns and $\tau_{3}~=~13.9$ ns, which were assigned to the photo produced excimer, monomer and excimer, respectively. Absorption and emission signals of aggregated samples at higher concentrations showed that inter-crystal excimer formed in tetrahydrfuran suspension were 20-times less than the one formed from a dilute solution.
## 3.4. MOFs possessing 1,8 and 2,7-naphthalenedicarboxylate linkers
There are less numbers of examples on the naphthalenedicarboxylate MOFs of the positional isomers other than the ones derived from 1,4- and 2,6-naphthalenedicatboxylate isomers; yet they provide very useful information to project their utilities. For example, metallacycles of 1,8-naphththalene dicarboxylates of cobalt, zinc, nickel was reported [83a]. The formation of rings of different nuclearity and sizes were dependent on the co-ligand as well as metal ions. For example, the hexa-nuclear and tetranuclear cobalt and nickel metallcycles were formed depending on use of $^{4,\acute{4}}$ -bpy or 1,10-phenanthroline as coligands. The tetraand hexa-nuclear $C0^{2+}$ -metallacycles were anti-ferromagnetic; whereas hexa-nuclear $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ metallacycle were having intramolecular ferromagnetic interactions [83a]. On the other hand, copper complex of bis( $\dot{\upmu}$ -naphthalene-1,8-dicarboxylato- $\kappa^{2}0^{1}{:}0^{8}$ )bis[aqu a-bis(N,N’-dimethylformamide- $^\kappa_{0}$ )copper(II)] was found to have distorted square pyramidal copper centers [82b].
Depending on the amounts of coordinated solvent and solvent of crystallisation, the 2,7-ndc based MOF of manganese, having trinuclear $[\mathsf{M n}_{3}(2,7\mathrm{-ndc})_{3}(\mathsf{D M F})_{2}]$ SBU adopted different crystalline forms [84a]. It was also observed that the one having the coordinated solvent molecules did not have permanent porosity but when those solvent molecules were not coordinating to manganese, it showed permanent micro pores. There are also examples, where solvent removal caused instability to zinc MOF, one such example is the $[\mathrm{Zn}_{4}(2,7\mathrm{-ndc})_{4}(\mathrm{dabco})(\mathrm{OH}_{2})_{2}]$ framework, which was found to be unstable upon guest removal.
Gallium and aluminium based MOF of 2,3-ndc, $\mathsf{\Pi}[\mathsf{G a}_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(2,3-$ ndc)] $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ [43c] and similar MOFs of $\mathrm{\Delta}[\mathrm{Ga}(\mathrm{OH})(2,6\mathrm{-ndc})]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ and $([\mathsf{A l}(0\mathsf{H})(2,6\mathsf{-n d c})]\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}0)$ (MIL-69) were studied for nitrogen absorptions and aquations [85]. Structural study had revealed reversible dehydration of MOFs $\mathrm{[Ga_{2}(O H)_{4}(2,3-n d c)]\cdot H_{2}O}$ and [Ga (OH)(2,6-ndc)] $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ . Due to non-porous nature these were inefficient to absorb nitrogen gas. These two MOFs are isoreticular to $([\mathsf{A l}_{2}(\mathsf{O H})_{4}(2,3\mathrm{-bdc})]\cdot\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O}$ (CAU-15) and [Al(OH)(2,6-ndc)] $\cdot\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ (MIL-69), respectively [43c]. The $\left[\mathsf{A l}(\mathsf{O H})(1,8\mathrm{-ndc})\right]$ and $[\mathsf{A l}_{2}(-$ $\mathrm{OH})_{4}(2,3\mathrm{-ndc})]{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ are isostructural to the corresponding gallium compounds. The [Al(OH)(1,8-ndc)] and $\mathrm{[Al_{2}(O H)_{4}(2,3\mathrm{-ndc})]\cdot H_{2}O}$ underwent reversible hydration and dehydration, but they did not adsorb nitrogen [85]. When additional functional groups were added to such MOFs the incorporation imparted additional auxochromes, controlled pore sizes, solubility, enhance catalytic activities. For example, nitro-naphthalene-3,7-dicarboxylate based $\mathsf{M O F-}205\mathsf{-N O_{2}}$ showed a lesser amount of hydrogen adsorption than the 1,5-dibenzyloxy-2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate MOF (MOF-205-OBn). The MOF-205-OBn showed a higher selectivity value of 6.5 for adsorption of $\mathsf{C O}_{2}/\mathsf{N}_{2}$ and 2.7 for adsorption of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ [86].
## 4. Some applied aspects of naphthalenedicarboxylate MOFs
In the foregoing discussions on the illustrations of specific structural material characteristics and further illustration in the table it is clear that naphthalenedicarboxylates are advance materials with wide applications. There exist large avenues to study the fundamental aspects and applications of the ndc based MOFs; some of those are listed in Fig. 18. These properties include node and linker modulation, hydrophobic effects, nano-sheet modulations, magnetic, electrical properties, antenna effects, catalysis, sensing, health and environment.
The IRMOF-8 is useful in fuel gas storage such as hydrogen and methane and also has high the ability to separate the ethylene and ethane gases from mixture [87]. Among the different IRMOF8 relatively lesser intrinsic surface area and pore volume of IRMOF-8 with respect to common IRMOFs it showed strong interactions towards methane; possibly of due to weak interactions interactions with extended $\pi$ -system. The continued efforts to enhance the hydrogen uptake by this MOF have shown significant improvement to use simplified synthetic procedures.
For such studies with IRMOF-8 there exists scopes to develop modified MOFs by exchanging linkers and by preparing hybrid materials [88]. The exchange of benzenedicarboxylic acids with different amounts of 1,4-naphthalenedicaroxylic acid were achieved to obtain single phase by using different ratios of the two linkers [88a]. These mixed linker MOFs have showed superior hydrogen uptake capabilities. The UiO-66 phase topology is conserved at all relative fractions of BDC/NDC. The comparison between the synchrotron radiation PXRD and 77 K $\Nu_{2}$ -adsorption isotherms obtained on the 50:50 BDC/NDC sample and on a mechanical mixture of the pure BDC and NDC samples univocally proves that in the mixed linkers of the MOFs the BDC and NDC linkers are shared in each MOF crystal. On the other hand, the UiO-66 type mixed linker of 1,4-benzenedicaboxylate and 1,4- naphthalenedicarboxylate MOF upon addition of benzoic acid or 3-phosphonopropionic acid additive [88b] causes significant changes in porosity. For example, depending on the amount of benzoic acid enhancement in porosity and also of ratio of the NDC/BDC originally present as linkers were observed and clearly showed the possibility of post-synthetic modifications by multiple types of carboxylates and also caused repair of the defect pores of UiO-66 type MOFs for enhanced performances.
Fenton reaction coupled with non-toxic MOFs in chemodynamic therapy provides improved actions [89a]. Porous MOF with tuneable near-IR emission properties as a function of lanthanide the metal ions are useful for in vivo bio imaging [89b,89c,89d]. IRMOF-3 incorporated with magnetic iron oxide nanoparticles is capable of delivery of drug folic acid towards cancer cells.88d Such studies suggest that correct selection of analogous naphthalendicarboxyate based MOFs may have similar applications. The copper(II)-copper(I) redox process of copper(II)- 1,4-naphthalenedicarboxylate nano-wires deposited on the surface of a copper foam acts as electrochemical immunosensor for the prostate specific antigen operating at required limit clinical diagnostic purpose [89e]. Photo responsive dyes have been utilized as imaging and photodynamic/photothermal therapy agents [90]. Indocyanine green with metal organic framework MIL-100(Fe) has clinical applications in anticancer photothermal therapy. IRMOF based MOFs modified with suitable linkers suggests their possible utility in delivery of anticancer agent tamoxifen and for drug like gemcitabine.
The inert gas absorption is an interesting applied side of ndcMOFs [3a,64c]. The Al-MOF [Al(OH)(1,4-ndc)] efficiently absorb inert gas xenon [3a]. This MOF has two different types of channels having diameter $7.7\times7.7\mathrm{~\AA^{2}}$ and $3.0\times3.0\mathrm{~\AA~}$ respectively. It has high thermal stability and permanent porosity. Continuous-flow hyperpolarized $^{129}\mathrm{Xe}$ NMR spectra have established that the larger channels of the MOF are partially occupied by xenon atoms at low pressure. Xenon atoms can diffuse to the large channels due to conducive size of xenon with respect to the larger pores but they are big enough to diffuse to channels with smaller diameter as illustrated in the Fig. 19. The wall of the channels having naphthalene rings provide interacting space for selective adsorption and intrinsic hydrophobicity allowing selective absorption of methanol and acetone over water.
The naphthalenedicarboxylate MOFs have shown promise in selective catalysis [91]. The gated behaviour of IRMOF 8 has enabled selective catalysis of the tert-butylation reaction of toluene [89b]. In general, an aluminium trichloride promoted Freidel-Craft t-butylation reaction of toluene produces higher amounts of o-substituted products, whereas, the use of IRMOF8 as catalyst had provided significant p-isomer selectively and it also inhibited the diarylated products those were produced in conventional Friedel-Craft reactions. In these examples, the pore windows are sufficiently large and could allow free passes of bulky products. Thus, it was not for the hindrance of the diffusion bulky products these selective catalytic reactions had occurred. Hence, the selectivity observed was due to the absorbance of reactant molecules in a particular orientation to form the suitable transition state leading to selective product. After mono-alkylation similar transition state was not possible because of steric hindrance and dialkylation did not occur. It may be noted that, a catalytic reaction may occur inside or outside pores/channels of a MOF; enhanced surface area and active sites are two parameters added together with the stabilisation of reactive species. Furthermore, the diffusion control of the reactants and products are important in catalytic reactions. As seen in some ndc based MOFs possess gated pores, multiple channels and have flexible structures that can change during sorption. These properties make the ndc-based MOF as prospective catalyst candidates. The rotation of aromatic part and use of functional groups with rotor-like motion would provide new prospective candidates for selective delivery and catalytic activities by this class of MOFs. MOFs having multiple types of dissimilar metal sites have shown promise to provide extra stability and performance over the conventional counter-part comprising of single metal nodes [92]. Those provide wide scopes to tune structures, properties and reactivities. Though such systems have shown promise in tandem catalysis and material properties, yet the scope of having polyaromatic linkers such as naphthalenediacarboxylate in such systems are yet to make a headway. So is the case in constructions of mixed isomeric naphthalenedicarboxylates to modulate structure and properties. The linker variations to achieve size and shape selective hosts will continue to draw interest. Food safety, preservations are of concerns to human health where the use of MOFs have shown promises, ndc based MOFs have already made a mark. For example, the control release of ethylene gas by IRMOF8 had been used in food preservation [93].
## 5. Conclusion
The naphthalenedicarboxylates have wide ranges of applications that were successfully designed in understanding of Nanoscale materials through molecular understanding. As illustrated in Fig. 18 there exist wide contributions to this class of MOFs as the fundamental aspects to deal for upcoming applications to environment, medical, energy sectors. Different principles based on gated pores, intrinsic acid-base properties, hydrophilic, hydrophobic pores, flexible part of linkers, modifications of nodes are the basis for reticular chemistry of ndc-based MOFs. The promising aspects generated from the orientations of the naphthalene units have provided control of optical properties, selective adsorptions, and stimuli guided properties. Whether it is the optoelectronics, energy storage, utility in medical sciences or biomimicking the role of the naphthalene linker guided processes will continue to provide large numbers of variations and eventually these are reflected in the trends in numbers of publications on those topics in recent years. The fabrication of devices and utility in biological sciences, catalysis, and stimuli guided processes from nano-composites of naphthalene derived linkers will continue to attract attention in recent future. Correlations between morphological and topological analysis in a generalised manner is still lacking, unified study of these two aspects would make major understandings to unearth the relationships of topology and morphology if any. There are avenues to having gated MOFs with multiple pores and also to arrange aromatic planes of linkers inside as well as at outer peripheries to unearth novel properties. The agglomeration of guest molecules such as water cluster and inert gas clusters, clathrates inside pores will open newer-avenues to study.
